  zone-count : Integer = list-length zone-rows

  zone-at : Integer -> ZoneRow
  zone-at (i) = list-at zone-rows i

Section: Asking

 THE ID IS THE INDEX PLUS ONE, so that zero can mean "no zone chosen, just an
 offset". A stored zero is what a machine that only ever had a `CTZ1` record
 has, and it must not read as Midway.

  zone-id-of : Integer -> Integer
  zone-id-of (i) = i + 1

  zone-valid : Integer -> Boolean
  zone-valid (id) = id >= 1 & id <= zone-count

  zone-row-of : Integer -> ZoneRow
  zone-row-of (id) = zone-at (if zone-valid id then id - 1 else 0)

 AN ID IS POSITIONAL AND A NAME IS NOT, which is why anything that names a
 particular zone must come in through here. Inserting one row into the table
 shifts every id after it, and a caller holding a literal id then reads a
 different city while continuing to look correct: it happened the first time a
 row was added, to a test that had pinned London, Sydney and Kolkata by number
 and afterwards measured three other places while still reporting three of its
 four verdicts as passes.

 The stored record keeps the id and not the name, because a byte is a byte and
 the table is the only thing that turns one into the other. What must never
 hold a literal id is SOURCE.

  zone-find-at : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  zone-find-at (nm) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else if (zone-at i).zr-name == nm then zone-id-of i
    else zone-find-at nm (i + 1) n

  zone-find : Text -> Integer
  zone-find (nm) = zone-find-at nm 0 zone-count

 The offset a zone is at ON A GIVEN DATE, which is the only question worth
 asking of it. The date is the machine's civil date and the hour is its hour,
 which is what makes this answerable without knowing the answer first.

  zone-offset-on : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  zone-offset-on (id) (y) (m) (d) (hh) =
    if zone-valid id == False then 0
    else
      let r = zone-row-of id
      in r.zr-base + (if loc-dst-on (r.zr-rule) y m d hh then loc-dst-minutes (r.zr-rule) else 0)

  zone-is-summer : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  zone-is-summer (id) (y) (m) (d) (hh) =
    if zone-valid id == False then False
    else let r = zone-row-of id
    in loc-dst-on (r.zr-rule) y m d hh

 A zone's label carries the offset it is at NOW rather than its standard one,
 because a list that says London is UTC+00:00 in July is telling somebody in
 London that the list is wrong.

  zone-label : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  zone-label (id) (y) (m) (d) (hh) =
    if zone-valid id == False then "no zone"
    else (zone-row-of id).zr-city & "   " & loc-utc-text (zone-offset-on id y m d hh)

Section: Stripes

 THE MAP IS 24 BANDS AND THE TABLE IS NOT SORTED INTO THEM. Two zones an hour
 apart can share a stripe (Kathmandu at +05:45 rounds to 6) and a stripe can
 hold none. The walk is linear over the table rather than an index into it,
 because an index would have to be kept in step with the table by hand and the
 table is the thing most likely to be edited.

  zone-stripe-min : Integer = 0 - 12
  zone-stripe-max : Integer = 14

  zone-in-stripe : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  zone-in-stripe (s) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if (zone-at i).zr-stripe == s then zone-in-stripe s (i + 1) n (list-push acc (zone-id-of i))
    else zone-in-stripe s (i + 1) n acc

  zone-stripe-ids : Integer -> List Integer
  zone-stripe-ids (s) = zone-in-stripe s 0 zone-count []

  zone-stripe-count : Integer -> Integer
  zone-stripe-count (s) = list-length (zone-stripe-ids s)

 The first zone of a stripe is what a click on the map selects, which is why
 the table is ordered by population within a stripe: the answer to "I clicked
 the band over western Europe" should be Paris and not the Azores.

  zone-stripe-first : Integer -> Integer
  zone-stripe-first (s) =
    let ids = zone-stripe-ids s
    in if list-length ids == 0 then 0 else list-at ids 0

